{% extends "base.html.jinja" %}
{% block content %}
<h1>Privacy policy</h1>
{% if ga4_measurement_id %}
<p class="lede">
  The {{ site_name }} website uses Google Analytics 4 to count visits and see
  which pages are read, unless your browser sends Global Privacy Control or
  Do Not Track, or you opt out.
  {# correction 1 (apps coverage): lede begins #}
  The {{ site_name }} apps for iPhone (with Apple Watch and widgets) and for
  Android collect no data: no analytics, no tracking, no advertising and no
  account. Neither app includes analytics, advertising or crash-reporting code.
  The Android app does include two libraries from Google, described under
  <a href="#app">The apps</a>.
  {# correction 1 (apps coverage): lede ends #}
</p>
{% else %}
<p class="lede">
  Neither the {{ site_name }} website nor its apps for iPhone and Android collect
  personal data. There is no account, no analytics, no advertising, no
  tracking, and no third-party script on the website.
</p>
{% endif %}
<p>
  The website's water pages do carry plain links to Amazon.com that earn the
  owner a commission; they are links, not ads, scripts or trackers. See
  <a href="#affiliate">Gear links</a>.
</p>
{# correction 1 (apps coverage): scope line #}
<p class="muted">Last updated 2026-10-20. Covers this website and the {{ site_name }} apps for iPhone and Android.</p>

<h2 id="site">The website</h2>
{% if ga4_measurement_id %}
<ul>
  <li><strong>Google Analytics 4.</strong> Each page loads Google's analytics
      script (gtag.js, from googletagmanager.com). It records the page you
      view, the page you came from, your browser, device type, screen size
      and language, your approximate location (which Google works out from
      your IP address; Google Analytics 4 does not store the IP address
      itself), and interactions such as scrolling and clicks on links to
      other sites. It tells repeat visits apart with a random identifier kept
      in first-party <code>_ga</code> cookies.</li>
  <li><strong>Global Privacy Control and Do Not Track are honored.</strong>
      If your browser sends either signal, the analytics script is never
      loaded: nothing is sent to Google and no analytics cookie is set.</li>
{% if app_store_live %}
  <li><strong>App Store link clicks.</strong> When you click the App Store
      badge or an App Store link on this site, Google Analytics records that
      click with the page it was on (home, support, about or water) and
      whether it was the badge or a text link. The same rules apply: nothing
      is recorded if your browser sends Global Privacy Control or Do Not
      Track, or if you opted out. The iOS app itself sends nothing.</li>
{% endif %}
  <li id="opt-out"><strong>Opting out on this device.</strong> The
      &ldquo;Opt out of analytics&rdquo; button at the bottom of every page
      turns Google Analytics off in this browser. Every page you open
      afterwards checks for that choice before loading Google Analytics, and
      so does not load it at all. The choice is saved in this
      browser&rsquo;s local storage for this site, not in a cookie, and is
      never sent anywhere. The button then reads &ldquo;Opt back in&rdquo;;
      press it to undo the choice. Clearing this site&rsquo;s data in your
      browser clears the choice too. Google Analytics cookies set before you
      opted out stay in your browser until they expire or you clear them,
      but nothing sends them to Google while you are opted out.</li>
  <li><strong>The EEA, the UK and Switzerland:</strong> analytics cookies are
      denied by default there, using Google's Consent Mode. Google Analytics
      then sets no cookie and receives only cookieless measurements.</li>
  <li><strong>No advertising use.</strong> Google signals and ad
      personalization are turned off, and the advertising consent signals
      (ad storage, ad user data, ad personalization) are denied everywhere.
      There are no ads on this site; the gear links described under
      &ldquo;Gear links&rdquo; below are plain affiliate links.</li>
  <li><strong>Retention:</strong> Google Analytics keeps event-level data for
      14 months, then deletes it. Aggregated reports, such as page-view
      totals, are kept longer.</li>
  <li><strong>Google's terms:</strong>
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites">how
      Google uses information from sites that use its services</a> and
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy
      policy</a>. Google also offers an
      <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout">opt-out browser
      add-on</a> for Google Analytics.</li>
  <li>Calendar feeds (.ics) are plain files with no analytics; your calendar
      app downloads them directly. This site does not know who has
      subscribed.</li>
  <li>No account, no email address, no sign-up, anywhere on this site.</li>
  <li>The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. Like any web host, GitHub receives
      standard request information (IP address, the page requested, time,
      browser type) to serve pages &mdash; see GitHub's
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">privacy
      statement</a>.</li>
</ul>
{% else %}
<ul>
  <li>Every page is a plain static file. No cookies are set and no JavaScript
      runs.</li>
  <li>No page makes a request to any server other than the one serving it.</li>
  <li>Calendar feeds (.ics) are plain files; your calendar app downloads them
      directly. This site does not know who has subscribed.</li>
  <li>The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. Like any web host, GitHub receives
      standard request information (IP address, the page requested, time,
      browser type) to serve pages &mdash; see GitHub's
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">privacy
      statement</a>. This site adds no collection on top of that.</li>
</ul>
{% endif %}

<h2 id="affiliate">Gear links</h2>
<p>
  Each California water page has a short &ldquo;Gear for this water&rdquo;
  list of links that search Amazon.com. They are affiliate links: this site is
  a participant in the Amazon Services LLC Associates Program, so if you buy
  something after following one, the site&rsquo;s owner earns a commission,
  and it costs you nothing extra. {{ amazon_disclosure }}
  {{ gear_endorsement }}
</p>
<ul>
  <li>The links are on the website, never in the iOS app.</li>
  <li>They are ordinary links. Nothing loads from Amazon when a page opens,
      and this site adds no script, pixel or tracking for them.</li>
  <li>Once you click, Amazon&rsquo;s own site and privacy notice apply, and
      Amazon uses the link&rsquo;s tag to credit this site.{% if ga4_measurement_id %}
      The analytics described above can count a click on any link to another
      site, under the same opt-outs.{% endif %}</li>
  <li>The links never change what this site says about a water or a planting
      schedule.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="app">The apps</h2>
<ul>
  {# correction 1 (apps coverage): items begin #}
  <li><strong>No analytics:</strong> neither app has analytics, tracking,
      advertising or crash reporting.{% if ga4_measurement_id %} The website's
      Google Analytics does not run inside the apps.{% endif %}</li>
  <li><strong>iPhone, Apple Watch and widgets:</strong> the iPhone app, its
      Apple Watch app, widgets and watch complications use Apple's own
      frameworks and no third-party SDK. The widgets and complications show a
      copy of your favorites kept on your device and make no network
      request.</li>
  <li><strong>Android and its widget:</strong> the Android app includes two
      libraries from Google, and no other third-party code. Google Play
      Billing shows Google Play's purchase sheet, checks which purchases you
      own and confirms a purchase to Google Play. Google Play In-App Review
      can show Google Play's own review card; anything you enter there goes to
      Google Play, and the app does not receive it. These libraries talk to
      Google Play, not to the developer, and Google's
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">privacy policy</a> covers
      what they handle. The Android home-screen widget shows a copy of your
      favorites kept on your device and makes no network request.</li>
  <li><strong>Android backup:</strong> the Android app's data is excluded from
      cloud backup. A device-to-device transfer to a new phone can carry it.
      Besides the items listed under &ldquo;What stays on your device&rdquo;,
      the Android app keeps a small record of when it last asked for a
      review.</li>
  {# correction 1 (apps coverage): items end #}
  <li><strong>What stays on your device:</strong> the waters you favorite, the
      latest copy of the planting schedule, and a record of which listings the
      app has already alerted you about. These live only in the app's own
      storage on your device (on iPhone, also in your device backups, if you
      back up your phone). Deleting the app deletes them. They are never sent
      to the developer or anyone else.</li>
  {# correction 3 (request wording): item begins #}
  <li><strong>The one network request:</strong> each app downloads the public
      planting-schedule file this website publishes, from GitHub Pages. The
      request has no account, device or advertising identifier, and nothing
      about you or your favorites. It carries a fixed app name in its
      User-Agent header and, once the app already has a copy, a cache tag
      (ETag) that GitHub Pages issued for that file. Both are the same for
      every user, so nothing in the request distinguishes one user from
      another. As with any web request, the host sees the IP address it came
      from.</li>
  {# correction 3 (request wording): item ends #}
  {# correction 2 (link scope): item begins #}
  <li><strong>Links:</strong> links to CDFW's map, to CDFW's schedule and to
      the other source sites open in your browser, on those sites. CDFW and
      those sites have their own privacy policies, and this site's analytics
      does not apply to them.{% if ga4_measurement_id %} This site's Google
      Analytics applies to this site's own pages only, as described
      above.{% endif %}</li>
  {# correction 2 (link scope): item ends #}
  {# correction 1 (apps coverage): notifications and purchases begin #}
  <li><strong>Notifications:</strong> alerts are local notifications that your
      phone schedules on your device, by iOS or by Android. There is no push service
      and no device token.</li>
  <li><strong>Purchases:</strong> the one-time in-app purchase is handled
      entirely by Apple on iPhone and by Google Play on Android. The developer
      does not receive your name, email address or payment details. Apple and
      Google Play each keep their own record of your purchase under your
      account with them, on terms set by them; see
      <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/">Apple's privacy
      policy</a> and <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's
      privacy policy</a>.</li>
  {# correction 1 (apps coverage): notifications and purchases end #}
  <li><strong>App Store privacy label:</strong> Data Not Collected.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="children">Children</h2>
{% if ga4_measurement_id %}
<p>
  The app collects nothing from anyone, including children. The website's
  analytics are the same for every visitor and never ask for a name, age or
  contact details.
</p>
{% else %}
<p>Nothing is collected from anyone, including children.</p>
{% endif %}

<h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
<p>If this policy changes, this page changes, and the date above with it.</p>

<h2 id="contact">Questions</h2>
{% if support_email %}
<p>Email <a href="mailto:{{ support_email }}">{{ support_email }}</a>.</p>
{% else %}
<p>See <a href="{{ root }}support/">support &amp; FAQ</a>.</p>
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
